breaking news now the irish prime  minister  is to issue a formal apology after a  report published in the last few minutes  detailed the sheer scale of abuse that  took place at mother and baby homes in  ireland  over a 75-year period the five-year  investigation was prompted by the  discovery  of a mass grave of babies and children  in county galway  our island correspondent stephen murphy  joins me  now this is a long awaited long overdue  report  many argue um talk us through some of  its findings soon  yeah sure it's a report as you mentioned  that has literally just been published  in the last few minutes on the irish  government website  it's around three thousand pages long so  we're just skimming through it at the  moment but i mean  uh some of the findings and some of the  language contained in this report is  pretty damning stuff it's described in  ireland  it calls a cold harsh environment for  many but particularly  cold and harsh for women all women in  ireland  suffered serious discrimination and  women who gave birth outside marriage  were subject to what it calls  particularly harsh treatment  many of these women went into the 18  institutions covered  by this report over many decades they  were forced under  pressure from family societal factors  and of course the influence of the  catholic church  to give birth to babies within these  so-called mother and baby homes they  were then  separated from those children and those  children were often given out for  adoption  or fostering elsewhere sometimes as far  away as the united states  many of those children never saw their  families never saw their biological  parents again the report goes on to say  however that there's no evidence that  women were forced to enter these homes  by the church or state authorities and i  know for a fact that many  of the survivors will vehemently dispute  that we have heard stories of the last  day or two  of um the local parish priests coming to  a home in the 1940s or the 1950s  and telling the family that the daughter  of the family a young  pregnant but unmarried woman was causing  a scandal  one described how his mother had been  bicycled on the crossbars of a bike at  seven months pregnant uh 10 to 12 miles  to enter one of these homes and then the  baby  once it was born was taken away from her  never to be seen again that's what  you're dealing with  uh in this report now i think the  headline figure is the death toll  uh the report confirms that 9 000 irish  children died in these homes and  during the period study that at times  that was twice  the national average mortality rate as  you mentioned there this  commission was set up after uh  groundbreaking work done by the  historian catherine corliss at the site  of a home in tomb in county galway in  the west of the country  we caught up with her there a bit  earlier and asked her why so many  children died  i believe that with a bit of hygiene and  with a bit of care  quite a number of babies could be saved  but it seems they were just lumped in  together  a disease was ram went rampant from one  to the other  and um the bottom cause in my for my  research  i can say neglect big time needle actor  not caring  and you can see you can see the lack of  care of when to give them no funeral  no proper barrier so it just it screams  off just  pure neglect  there have been several interim reports  by this commission so we had an inkling  of  what was contained but there's no doubt  the the findings and the language in  this 3000 page report  uh is sure to be shocking to many as the  details emerge over the coming  uh minutes and hours we're just about to  have a press briefing here at government  buildings in dublin  uh from the taoiseach the irish prime  minister me hall martin to hear  more details of the state's response but  it is expected he will issue a state  a formal state apology when the doll the  irish parliament returns  tomorrow but for now there's no doubt  this is a another  grim recap of a dark chapter in modern  irish society
 

